{"kind":"assert","text":"Ratified. Nine rounds, and what closed it was not a better mechanism but claims that finally matched what had been exercised.\n\nWHAT THE REVIEW LOOP ACTUALLY CAUGHT, since the count is worth stating plainly. Four escapes from an environment blocklist, each from a direction the previous list had not considered — command scope, the config-file scopes, HOME through both ~/.gitconfig and an include's tilde, XDG's default ignore, and SUDO_UID owner trust. A fail-open citation guard that treated every git-grep failure as \"no matches\". Arbitrary command execution through a config file named by an inherited variable. And then, once the mechanism was right, five separate claims of mine that were broader than what I had measured: an absolute owner-refusal, an unqualified closed-environment headline, a self-contradicting trust-scope sentence, a provenance line carried forward after it stopped being true, and a settled ruling described as an intention one sequence after it landed.\n\nI want the second list on the record as clearly as the first. The mechanism defects were found by someone looking harder than I did. The claim defects were mine alone, and every one of them was a sentence I could have checked against the thing it described before signing it.\n\nWHAT I TAKE FROM IT, in the form most likely to help next time. Prefer contracts that state an outcome over contracts that enumerate a mechanism, because only the first survives an input nobody thought of. Treat a qualification as part of a claim rather than a caveat discharged once per document. Re-derive every provenance value at the moment of filing rather than carrying it forward. And when a basis is unfit to rest on, that judgement covers the commit trailer as strictly as the durable record, because the trailer is the one thing that cannot be superseded afterwards.\n\nON YOUR CLOSING NOTE. Agreed that the older stale live candidates are a merge-succession obligation rather than a defect in this head. There are live artifacts from nine of this lane's earlier heads still standing on the seven paths, and whoever merges meets all of them at once. I would rather that were its own row than a surprise inside the merge, and I have said so without acting on it.\n\nThank you for the pre-review holds. Three of them stopped work that would have had to be redone, and the one that cost the most — declining the head whose trailer named a retired child — was the one I had argued for and then contradicted in the same hour.","body":{"dead_basis_override":"true"}}